A||||11||frontal lobe||Engine stalled||||1||
Z||000000||frontal lobe||11-15-2001||02:02 AM||||'87GT v6 with 71,000 miles.  Bought it with 65,000 miles and no problems until this week.  Driving it for about 30 minutes.  At a stop light the engine just quits.  I crank but can't get it started.  Wait about 3-5 minutes, try it again and it starts right up and seems to run fine all the way home.  Has happened the last 2 times I've driven it now.<P>Common problem?  Easy fix?<P>Thanks in advance.||||reg||1
Z||000001||camon||11-15-2001||03:20 AM||||Sounds like you have something ignition related that is overheating. I would check for spark next time it stalls.<P>Never had this happen in my fiero but had a simular problem in my sidekick.......<P>Drove it for like an hour and then it just died. It started up again after I left it cool down. Well I put up with this for a few days. I was told to check the module. Well I didn't check it I just went out and bought a new one...threw it in the sidekick and it hasn't done it since (and that has been about 2 years now).<P>This may not be your problem but I had a very simular problem and that's what it was in my case!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||3
Z||000002||tgowens||11-15-2001||05:16 AM||||sounds like an ignition module....... that would be my first guess (if that isn't it, I'll guess again!)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||11-15-2001||12:42 PM||||Probably your timing.  Is the stoplight where the stalling occurs, at the edge of a time zone?  If so, try reseting the clock.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Seriously, the thermal theories posted earlier make sense to me, too, FWIW.  The engine compartment temperature tends to rise when you sit at a stoplight, so that may be what pushes a marginal ignition module over the edge.  Might want to check to see if your radiator fan comes on when it's supposed to, also. <P>Glad to see you've "moved up" to a GT! My own 1987 GT has 200k+ miles on it and going strong - hope yours treats you as well as mine has me.  Good luck with it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P><BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-15-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000004||frontal lobe||11-15-2001||02:39 PM||||Thank you for the replies.<P>What should I expect to pay for an ignition module?  (Here's my automotive repair experience:  replaced passenger headlight using ogre's recommended technique, and installed radio/cd instead of the old stock one.  In other words, I don't know what I'm doing.)  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||DR650SE||11-15-2001||02:52 PM||||Also if you have low oil it shuts of the fuel pump.  Happened to me pullin some g's  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>AIM:  DR650SE<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||1
Z||000006||frontal lobe||11-15-2001||06:37 PM||||Hey, I know how to check that!<P>I'll check.  It had been fine a couple of weeks ago.||||reg||1
Z||000007||camon||11-15-2001||11:17 PM||||Ignition module for the Fiero is around 30 or 40 bucks at autozone (if memory serves me correct). I never replaced one in the fiero but I have read the procedure on the web and seems pretty straight forward and easy (sounds like about a half hour job.... if that). <P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000008||tgowens||11-16-2001||02:31 AM||||Keith is close...... ignition module will be around $32 and it's about five minutes to change it -- remove the distributor cap, take out the two screws holding the ignition module to the distributor, remove the plug from pick-up coil to ignition module and the two plugs into the ignition module, reverse procedure to install the new one and .... voila -- it shouldn't quit on you at the next red light......||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fiero_24||11-16-2001||08:43 AM||||I agree on the module.  I had the same problem until it just died one day.  It is a heat related problem, so do not get the part tested.  SInce it will be out of the car you will not get an acurate test.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||7
Z||000010||frontal lobe||11-17-2001||07:08 PM||||I was in the zone today...autozone.<P>$25 for the ignition module, and since I was doing it, sprung for another $8 for a distributor cap.  <P>Public thanks to tgowens for the instructions, since I have ZERO mechanical background.  Also, thanks to the others who had the same thought.  I had actually called a local mechanic to have him look at it, and he mentioned on the phone that maybe it was the ignition module.<P>Installation went fine.  Drove it for 45 minutes afterward, with no stalling.<P>Now, is it not stalling because the problem is fixed, or because it just didn't happen????  Hard to prove a negative.  Will just have to drive it, and the more times I do and it doesn't stall, the more convinced I'll be!<P>NOW, how hard is it to change the spark plug wires???  One was in pretty bad shape... would hardly stay seated on the distributor post.  And just go back to autozone and get any set?  Cost?||||reg||1
Z||000011||camon||11-17-2001||08:53 PM||||Wires are a breeze to install (they were for me anyway).I upgraded to 8mm wires when I put in the accel coil. They weren't hard at all to install. <BR>****Just install them one at a time (take one off and replace it with the new one then move on to the next one...this way you won't mix up the wires).*** <P>Wires will cost different prices depending on what you get. the stock (plain jane) wires will cost like 20 bucks (give or take a few bucks) those are the 7mm wires (just ask the autozone guy for the wires).<P>I would also suggest changing plugs if they haven't been changed in awhile. Get rapid fires or platium +4's if you can spare the extra 2-3 bucks per plug (they cost about 5.00 a plug). <P>Side note:<BR>Don't know if you tried changing plugs or not but it isn't as hard as some people report (well the first time will have you cursing alittle...maybe). I used a deep dish 5/8" socket and a small extension and I had my rear plugs (the ones on the firewall side of the engine) out in about 5 minutes and the new ones in. I did take off both engine bay vents and it gave me more room to work!<P>Just a thought! <P>------------------<BR>Keith<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 11-17-2001).]||||reg||1
